var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Usage","page":"Usage","text":"KDL is a node-oriented document language. A document is a sequence of nodes, and each node has a name, then arguments and properties, then an optional block of child nodes:\n\nnode example=#true {\n    foo 1 2 3\n    bar 4 5 6\n}\n\nArguments are position-dependent values (1 2 3), properties are position-independent assigned values (example=#true), and children are full nodes. Values parse to Julia values: strings to String, numbers to Int or Float64, #true and #false to Bool, and #null to nothing.","title":"Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Parsing","page":"Usage","text":"The entry point is kdl. It reads a string, or a string literal:\n\njulia> doc = kdl(\"foo 1 2 key=3\");\n\njulia> doc = kdl\"foo 1 2 key=3\";\n\njulia> doc isa KDLNode\ntrue\n\n... a file, or an open stream:\n\njulia> path = tempname();\n\njulia> write(path, \"foo 1 2 key=3\");\n\njulia> KDLDocs.kdl_read(path) isa KDLNode\ntrue\n\njulia> open(io -> kdl(io), path) isa KDLNode\ntrue\n\nInvalid documents raise a KDLParseError:\n\njulia> try kdl(\"foo =\") catch e; e isa KDLParseError end\ntrue","title":"Parsing"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Children","page":"Usage","text":"Child nodes nest inside a node's braces, and they are stored by name. Reading a name as a field returns the child; for duplicate names, the last occurrence:\n\njulia> doc = kdl\"\"\"\n       package {\n           name my_pkg\n           dependencies {\n               lodash optional=#true config=9.3\n           }\n       }\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> doc.package isa KDLNode\ntrue\n\njulia> doc.package.dependencies.lodash[:optional]\ntrue\n\nA child whose name is not a Julia identifier is read with var\"...\":\n\njulia> doc = kdl(\"\\\"my node\\\" 1\");\n\njulia> doc.var\"my node\" isa KDLNode\ntrue\n\nAn absent child raises a FieldError, so test with haskey first:\n\njulia> doc = kdl\"\"\"\n       package {\n           name my_pkg\n       }\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> haskey(doc.package, :name)\ntrue\n\npropertynames lists the child names, and children returns the ordered (name, node) pairs:\n\njulia> doc = kdl\"\"\"\n       package {\n           name my_pkg\n       }\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> propertynames(doc.package)\n(:name,)\n\njulia> [name for (name, _) in children(doc)] == [:package]\ntrue\n\nAssign a field to add or replace a child:\n\njulia> doc = kdl\"\"\"\n       package {\n           name my_pkg\n       }\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> doc.package.new_child = KDLNode();\n\njulia> doc.package.new_child isa KDLNode\ntrue","title":"Children"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Arguments-and-properties","page":"Usage","text":"Arguments are ordered and unnamed; properties are keyed. Arguments read by integer index, and in tests membership:\n\njulia> node = kdl(\"foo 1 key=val 3\").foo;\n\njulia> arguments(node) == [1, 3]\ntrue\n\njulia> node[1]\n1\n\njulia> 1 in node\ntrue\n\njulia> :val in node\nfalse\n\nArgument order is significant, and duplicate arguments are preserved:\n\njulia> arguments(kdl(\"node arg arg\").node) == [:arg, :arg]\ntrue\n\nProperties read by index. An absent property is missing, so a property set to #false stays distinct from an absent one:\n\njulia> node = kdl(\"foo flag=#false\").foo;\n\njulia> node[:flag]\nfalse\n\njulia> node[:nope]\nmissing\n\njulia> haskey(node, :flag)\ntrue\n\nSet a property by assignment:\n\njulia> node = kdl(\"foo flag=#false\").foo;\n\njulia> node[:flag] = true\ntrue\n\njulia> node[:flag]\ntrue\n\nmissing is not a KDL value, so assigning it is an error:\n\njulia> node = kdl(\"foo flag=#false\").foo;\n\njulia> try node[:nope] = missing catch e; e isa ArgumentError end\ntrue\n\nDelete a property through properties(node);\n\njulia> node = kdl(\"foo flag=#false\").foo;\n\njulia> delete!(properties(node), :flag);\n\njulia> node[:flag]\nmissing\n\nA key that is not a Julia identifier needs the string form. A Symbol and its string form name the same property:\n\njulia> node = kdl(\"node my-key=1 \\\"my key\\\"=2\").node;\n\njulia> node[\"my-key\"]\n1\n\njulia> node[\"my key\"]\n2","title":"Arguments and properties"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Adding-arguments","page":"Usage","text":"push! appends a positional argument, and integer assignment replaces one:\n\njulia> node = kdl(\"node 1 2\").node;\n\njulia> push!(node, 3);\n\njulia> node[1] = 9;\n\njulia> arguments(node) == [9, 2, 3]\ntrue","title":"Adding arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Duplicate-node-names","page":"Usage","text":"A document may hold sibling nodes with the same name. A field read returns the last occurrence, while children keeps every occurrence in document order:\n\njulia> doc = kdl\"\"\"\n       node\n       node\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> length(children(doc))\n2\n\njulia> doc.node === last(children(doc))[2]\ntrue","title":"Duplicate node names"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Serialisation","page":"Usage","text":"show prints a node as KDL, and the output of a parsed document round-trips through kdl:\n\njulia> doc = kdl\"\"\"\n       foo 1 2 key=3 {\n           bar\n       }\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> kdl(sprint(show, doc)) isa KDLNode\ntrue\n\nwrite writes the same serialisation to an IO:\n\njulia> doc = kdl(\"foo 1 2 key=3\");\n\njulia> path = tempname();\n\njulia> open(io -> write(io, doc), path, \"w\");\n\njulia> KDLDocs.kdl_read(path) isa KDLNode\ntrue","title":"Serialisation"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Merging","page":"Usage","text":"merge and merge! combine nodes:\n\njulia> a = kdl(\"foo 1\");\n\njulia> b = kdl(\"bar 2\");\n\njulia> m = merge(a, b);\n\njulia> arguments(m.foo) == [1] && arguments(m.bar) == [2]\ntrue","title":"Merging"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Nodes","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Collections","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Collections"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Parsing","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Parsing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Navigating-and-reading","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Navigating and reading"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Mutating","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Mutating"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Serialising","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Serialising"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#KDLDocs.KDLNode","page":"API Reference","text":"KDLNode\n\nA node in a parsed KDL document.\n\nA node has:\n\narguments: an ordered Vector of positional argument values – duplicates and order are preserved;\nproperties: an OrderedDict of key => value pairs (rightmost wins);\nbody: an OrderedDict of children keyed by name (last occurrence wins);\nchildren: an ordered Vector of (name, node) pairs – duplicates and order are preserved.\n\nReach the collections with arguments, properties and children. k[:name] reaches a child, k[1] an argument by position, k.name a property value (missing when absent), and name in k tests argument membership.  show serialises a node back to KDL.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"KDLDocs.KDLNode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#KDLDocs.arguments","page":"API Reference","text":"arguments(k::KDLNode)\n\nThe ordered positional arguments of k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"KDLDocs.arguments"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#KDLDocs.properties","page":"API Reference","text":"properties(k::KDLNode)\n\nThe properties of k, as an OrderedDict of key => value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"KDLDocs.properties"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#KDLDocs.children","page":"API Reference","text":"children(k::KDLNode)\n\nThe children of k, as an ordered Vector of (name, node) pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"KDLDocs.children"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#KDLDocs.kdl","page":"API Reference","text":"kdl(x)\n\nParse x into a KDLNode, or throw KDLParseError if x is not valid.\n\nx may be a String or an IO; the latter will be read in full.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"KDLDocs.kdl"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#KDLDocs.@kdl_str","page":"API Reference","text":"@kdl_str \"...\"\n\nParse the KDL document in the string literal at macro-expansion time.  A non-standard string literal passes Julia escape sequences through unchanged apart from the escaped quote, so terminate nodes with a semicolon or use the triple-quoted form for a multi-line document.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"KDLDocs.@kdl_str"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#KDLDocs.kdl_read","page":"API Reference","text":"kdl_read(path)\n\nParse the KDL document stored in the file at path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"KDLDocs.kdl_read"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#KDLDocs.KDLParseError","page":"API Reference","text":"KDLParseError(msg)\n\nRaised when a KDL document cannot be parsed, or violates a semantic rule of the specification (reserved identifier, invalid escape, multi-line string structure, disallowed code point, ...).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"KDLDocs.KDLParseError"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.getindex-Tuple{KDLNode, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"k[i::Integer]\n\nThe i-th positional argument of k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getindex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.getindex-Tuple{KDLNode, Union{AbstractString, Symbol}}","page":"API Reference","text":"k[key]\n\nThe value of the property key of k, or missing when absent.  key may be a Symbol or a String, and the two forms name the same property.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getindex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.getproperty-Tuple{KDLNode, Symbol}","page":"API Reference","text":"k.name\n\nThe child node named name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getproperty"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.propertynames-Tuple{KDLNode}","page":"API Reference","text":"propertynames(k::KDLNode)\n\nThe child names of k, as a Tuple.  Drives tab completion on a parsed node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.propertynames"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.in-Tuple{Any, KDLNode}","page":"API Reference","text":"x in k::KDLNode\n\nWhether x is one of the positional arguments of k.  Membership uses isequal, so missing in k is false and NaN in k works.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.in"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.haskey-Tuple{KDLNode, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"haskey(k::KDLNode, i)\n\nWhether k has a child or property named i.  Positional arguments are unnamed; test them with i in k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.haskey"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.setindex!-Tuple{KDLNode, Any, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"k[i::Integer] = v\n\nReplace the i-th positional argument of k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.setindex!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.setindex!-Tuple{KDLNode, Any, Union{AbstractString, Symbol}}","page":"API Reference","text":"k[key] = v\n\nSet the property key of k to v.  A missing value is rejected, since KDL has no missing value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.setindex!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.setproperty!-Tuple{KDLNode, Symbol, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"k.name = node\n\nAdd or replace the child node named name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.setproperty!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.push!-Tuple{KDLNode, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"push!(k::KDLNode, v)\n\nAppend the positional argument v to k, returning k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.push!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.empty!-Tuple{KDLNode}","page":"API Reference","text":"empty!(k::KDLNode)\n\nRemove all arguments, properties and children of k, returning k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.empty!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.merge-Tuple{KDLNode, KDLNode}","page":"API Reference","text":"merge(j::KDLNode, k::KDLNode)\n\nA new node combining the arguments, properties and children of j and k. Arguments and children are concatenated; properties and name-keyed children are merged with k winning on clashes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.merge"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.merge!-Tuple{KDLNode, KDLNode}","page":"API Reference","text":"merge!(j::KDLNode, k::KDLNode)\n\nMerge k into j in place and return j.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.merge!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.show-Tuple{IO, KDLNode}","page":"API Reference","text":"show(io, k::KDLNode)\n\nSerialise the node k back to KDL.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.show"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.write-Tuple{IO, KDLNode}","page":"API Reference","text":"write(io::IO, k::KDLNode)\n\nWrite the KDL serialisation of k to io.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.write"},{"category":"section","location":"#KDLDocs.jl","page":"Home","text":"KDL Documents in Julia.\n\nKDL (KDL Document Language) is a node-oriented document language similar to XML and JSON. This package implements the KDL 2.0 specification, using the PikaMacros / PikaParser parser toolkit.","title":"KDLDocs.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"julia> doc = kdl\"\"\"\n       node example=#true {\n           foo 1 2 3\n           bar 4 5 6\n       }\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> doc.node[:example]\ntrue\n\njulia> doc.node.foo[1]\n1\n\njulia> arguments(doc.node.foo)\n3-element Vector{Any}:\n 1\n 2\n 3\n\nEach KDL node has three kinds of field: arguments, properties, and children. Arguments and properties sit before the optional block; arguments are position-dependent values (1 2 3 in foo 1 2 3), while properties are position-independent assigned values (example=#true). Children are full nodes, and they nest inside the braces.\n\nkdl() parses a document into a KDLNode. Children read as fields (doc.node), properties and arguments by index (doc.node[:example], doc.node.foo[1]), and an absent property is missing. Base.show serialises a node back to KDL, so the printed form round-trips:\n\njulia> doc = kdl\"\"\"\n       node example=#true {\n           foo 1 2 3\n       }\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> kdl(sprint(show, doc)) isa KDLNode\ntrue","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Pages","page":"Home","text":"Usage – parsing, navigation, mutation, serialisation, and errors.\nAPI Reference – the documented public interface.","title":"Pages"}]
}
